# KNOWN GAP — ICE: calling a `Callable[...]`-typed LOCAL VARIABLE with a
# CONSUMING argument panics the Rust lowerer.
#
#   thread 'gg-main' panicked at src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:2114:
#   Tier 2a consume-site violation: 1 violation(s) in module '<unknown>'.
#   First: fn @main bb0 i5 — CollectionMutator(__gorget_closure_call_3,
#   arg #0) — untracked source consumed (ownership not decided).
#
# `gg check` passes cleanly; the panic is at IR lowering, so this is a
# crash-on-valid-program, not a rejected program.
#
# GLYPH-INDEPENDENT: the retired-glyph twin (`String !s` in the signature,
# `Callable[void(int &, String !)]` in the type, `f(&a, !greeting)` at the
# call) panics with the BYTE-IDENTICAL message. So this is NOT a D27
# accept-both issue — it is an ownership-tracking hole for the
# closure-call path.
#
# DISCRIMINATOR vs the working case: the same program with an INDEXED callee
# compiles and runs fine —
#   Vector[Callable[void(int &, String ^)]] arr = []
#   arr.push(mix)
#   arr[0](&a, ^greeting)
# — which is exactly `callable_bang_arr_indexed_callee.gg`, the one in-corpus
# cell of that shape. So the hole is specific to calling a Callable-typed
# LOCAL BINDING, not to Callable calls in general: the indexed form registers
# the consume site, the bare-variable form does not.
#
# ⚠ `d27_sh_caret_fntype_param_suffix.gg` is NOT a caret twin of the indexed
# shape — it passes its callable as a PARAMETER, and shares only the fn-type
# param-suffix parse position. No caret indexed-callee cell exists. (The
# indexed shape also runs on the Rust lane ONLY: it times out on the self-host
# runtime lane, filed separately.)
#
# INTENDED behaviour: this compiles and runs, printing the two lines below.
# Per Core #3 the fix belongs at the PRODUCER — the closure-call lowering
# must register the argument's ownership at the consume site, the same way
# the indexed-callee path already does — not at the validator's read site.
#
# Expected stdout: hi\n101

void mix(int &n, String ^s):
    n = n + 100
    print(s)

void main():
    int a = 1
    String greeting = "hi"
    Callable[void(int &, String ^)] f = mix
    f(&a, ^greeting)
    print(a)
